<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Potawatomi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Potawatomi are an Anishinaabe people of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a>, closely related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawa">Odawa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ojibwe">Ojibwe</a>, who in the 18th century lived around the southern shores of Lake Michigan. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> of the French, they later joined the Western Confederacy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tecumseh">Tecumseh</a>'s Confederacy against American expansion. Under the removal policy of the 1830s most were forced west to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kansas">Kansas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>, while some remained in Michigan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/potawatomi.jpg" alt="Potawatomi" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author or not provided · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Potawatomi_1920.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Potawatomi call themselves Neshnabé, a cognate of Anishinaabe, meaning original people. They are part of a long-term alliance, the Council of Three Fires, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ojibwe">Ojibwe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawa">Odawa</a>, and are regarded as its youngest brother. In that context they are called Bodéwadmi, keepers of the fire, because they tend the council fire.</p><p>They traditionally speak Potawatomi, a Central Algonquian language most similar to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawa">Odawa</a>. Children are instructed in the Seven Grandfather Teachings of wisdom, respect, love, honesty, humility, bravery and truth, and in the story of the Water Spider, who weaves a bowl from her web and sails across the water to bring back fire for the other animals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Early 17th-century French records suggest that the Potawatomi lived in what is now southwestern Michigan. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaver-wars">Beaver Wars</a> they fled to the area around Green Bay to escape the expansion of the Iroquois and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neutral-confederacy">Neutral Confederacy</a>, and they numbered about 3,000 in 1658. Historians divide their post-contact history into French contact, British contact, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> treaty period and Removal. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> ceded its lands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, the Potawatomi took every British frontier garrison except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/detroit">Detroit</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontiac">Pontiac</a>'s Rebellion, and they expanded west from Detroit and south along the shore of Lake Michigan.</p><p>They were an important part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tecumseh">Tecumseh</a>'s Confederacy, fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tecumsehs-war">Tecumseh's War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-1812">War of 1812</a>, and switched sides between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> as power and their trade and land interests shifted. Chiefs Blackbird and Nuscotomeg led about 500 warriors against the evacuation column at the Battle of Fort Dearborn, killing most of the civilians and 54 of Captain Nathan Heald's force, while Mucktypoke had counselled against the attack and later saved some captives.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Treaties of the late 1820s created reservations, and in the Second Treaty of Prairie du Chien of 1829 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ojibwe">Chippewa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottawa">Ottawa</a> and Potawatomi ceded most of their lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a> and Michigan. By the Treaty of Chicago of 1833 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> promised new lands, annuities and supplies in return for further cessions.</p><p>The Illinois Potawatomi were removed to Nebraska and the Indiana Potawatomi to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kansas">Kansas</a>, and those in Kansas were later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a>. Annuities were often reduced or late, and many suffered. The Catholic priest Benjamin Petit documented the Trail of Death of 1838. Many Potawatomi, mostly in Michigan, found ways to stay, and others fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawa">Odawa</a> or to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>.</p><p>Today federally recognised Potawatomi tribes include the Forest County Potawatomi Community, the Hannahville Indian Community, the Pokagon Band, the Prairie Band of Potawatomi Nation and the Citizen Potawatomi Nation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>, and communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>. Most first-language speakers are elderly, and the people are working to revitalise the language. Place names such as Waukegan, Muskegon, Kalamazoo and Skokie come from it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Potawatomi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The United States proclaimed the Treaty of St. Louis with the Council of Three Fires, the Ottawa, Ojibwe and <b>Potawatomi</b> of the Illinois and Milwaukee rivers.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-30">30 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the War of 1812, warriors under the <b>Potawatomi</b> chiefs Winamac and Five Medals killed two men of the U.S. garrison at Fort Wayne, beginning a siege by Potawatomi and Miami forces.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-5">5 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The confederacy united warriors from the Shawnee, Kickapoo, <b>Potawatomi</b>, Wyandot, Ho-Chunk and others under Tecumseh's leadership and his brother's religious authority.</blockquote><a href="/state/tecumsehs-confederacy">Tecumseh's Confederacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Building on earlier alliances, Shawnee, Miami, Lenape, Wyandot, Odawa, Ojibwe, <b>Potawatomi</b> and others coordinated through councils, with support from the Mohawk leader Joseph Brant, insisting on the Ohio as the boundary.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-confederacy">Western Confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
